      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the landmark case of Thorne v. Maritime Logistics Ltd, the Court of Appeal decisively clarified the extent of employer liability, rejecting the theory that a duty of care could be imposed on sub-contracted workers based on the premises of 'vicarious proximity.' The appellant cited Smith v. Jones (2018), advocating for the control test's applicability which arguably simplifies determining employer responsibility. Contrarily, the court drew upon Henderson v. Global Shipping (2020) to elucidate how modern logistics dilute conventional supervision and therefore limit vicarious liability. Furthermore, the fundamental concept articulated in Miller v. Transport Corp (2019) – that proximity must include both physical and legal ties – was fundamental in dismissing the appellant's case. Building on this foundation, the court applied the refined analysis to affirm that the enormity of control mandated in Smith v. Jones is incompatible with the fragmented, decentralized autonomy that prevails in contemporary logistics operations. Consequently, the delineation of control within vicarious liability, as discussed across these landmark cases, led to the appellant's case in Thorne v. Maritime Logistics Ltd ultimately being dismissed, as sub-contractor autonomy insulates the main contractor from vicarious responsibility. This ruling not only solidified existing",
      "source": "target",
